   CSS NOTES: Adjacent Sibling Combinator (+)

   Definition:
   The adjacent sibling combinator (+) selects an element that
   comes immediately after another element and shares the same parent.

   Syntax:
   A + B

   Meaning:
   Selects element B only if it directly follows element A.

   Rules:
   - Both elements must have the same parent
   - Only the immediate next sibling is selected
   - If any element comes in between, it will not work

   Example:
   h1 + p {
     color: red;
   }

   HTML:
   <h1>Heading</h1>
   <p>Selected paragraph</p>
   <p>Not selected</p>

   Explanation:
   Only the first <p> immediately after <h1> is selected.

   Use Cases:
   - Styling heading + paragraph
   - Adding spacing between consecutive elements
   - Simple layout adjustments

   Comparison:
   +  Adjacent sibling → only next element
   ~  General sibling  → all following siblings

   Summary:
   A + B selects B only when it immediately follows A.
